00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.920
So, Letitia put out a question about Eigenfaces:&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.600
While an eigen face can just be seen&nbsp;
as a weird german-english mixed word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.840
(fun fact: we call mutant words "denglish" in&nbsp;
german), it's also a way of analyzing faces.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.680
And while eigenfaces are some&nbsp;
more "classical" computer vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.000
invented before the dominance of neural&nbsp;
networks, they're still pretty interesting:

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.040
The idea is to describe a given&nbsp;
face through a combination of fixed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.360
components, to be more exact: a LINEAR&nbsp;
combination of some base face pieces.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.080
So to get Letitia's face, you could re-combine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.760
the face pieces from the learned&nbsp;
base to actually form her face.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.400
So, how do you do that? For a&nbsp;
given input collection of faces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:40.560
you represent them as vectors. Then, you&nbsp;
compute a Principal Component Analysis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.560
of this data – basically obtaining the&nbsp;
"most important" vectors that you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.240
to properly express the faces in the&nbsp;
collection as a linear combination.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.520
So for our faces given as a set of vectors, here&nbsp;
just shown with two dimensions (because drawing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.960
100,000 dimensions gets somewhat cumbersome) we&nbsp;
compute the covariance matrix, then compute the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:04.080
first k eigenvectors that we can then use as&nbsp;
a basis to approximate any input face vector.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.920
And this is why we call these base faces&nbsp;
the "eigenfaces" – because they are face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.720
components computed from eigenvectors of&nbsp;
the covariance matrix of all the faces.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.320
These Eigenfaces can then be used for&nbsp;
things like real-time face detection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.480
while they are still somewhat fast and robust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.840
at least when you compare that to other&nbsp;
approaches that are not Deep Learning-based.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.800
For the next question, I want to summarize&nbsp;
what domain adaptation is, and for clarity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.440
also what transfer learning is in&nbsp;
general is and how that differs.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:34.080
While that is a bit of a fuzzy definition,&nbsp;
we can generally say that domain adaptation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.120
happens when one solves the same task but&nbsp;
changes the kind of input the task requires.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.320
For example, we train to detect dogs in an image&nbsp;
taken during the day, and after domain adaptation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:48.240
we still try to detect dogs, but perhaps&nbsp;
now with images taken during the night.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.240
We just have a different, but still&nbsp;
related input data distribution.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.280
This domain adaptation is part of&nbsp;
what is called Transfer learning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:02.240
For transfer learning, not only the domain can&nbsp;
change, but the task itself can also change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:06.560
We repurpose a model trained on one&nbsp;
task to be used on a different one.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.080
So… "What [actually] changes during&nbsp;
domain adaptation between settings?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:14.880
Well… we just swap out the data distribution&nbsp;
of the input. And as you can already imagine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.000
the difference whether something is&nbsp;
just domain adaptation or already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.880
learning a different task is&nbsp;
somewhat flimsy and up for debate.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.920
Where is the line in all of this? Is detecting&nbsp;
dogs at night already a different task?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.960
Is detecting cats so much different&nbsp;
that it's not just a different data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.840
distribution, but essentially a new task?

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.760
[Causation]:
Let’s move on to another one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.520
which is trying to find out how you see one&nbsp;
of the latest trends in Machine Learning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:41.600
You were asked whether you believe that&nbsp;
causality will be "The next big thing™&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.800
in machine learning - and&nbsp;
roughly half of you thought so.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:48.640
So, what is causality in ML&nbsp;
about, and why do we need it?
 &nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.280
Well, machine learning is TERRIBLE&nbsp;
at knowing causal relationships:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.960
For example, when it's raining, there&nbsp;
more people outside holding umbrellas.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.680
Without more information, an&nbsp;
machine learning algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.640
looking at images could also come to think&nbsp;
using umbrellas actually causes it to rain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:09.360
The machine learning algorithm doesn't know how&nbsp;
our world works, it just detects patterns and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.960
correlations, and not causal relationships. And&nbsp;
correlation is not enough to establish causality.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:21.120
Well, how do humans overcome this? With trying&nbsp;
things out! Does it start pouring if you pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:26.720
out your umbrella while the sun is shining? Well,&nbsp;
no. It only causes people to stare at you weirdly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:30.720
but that is exactly the problem: this "trying&nbsp;
out", which is called an "intervention"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.560
in this causality context, is something&nbsp;
generally machine learning doesn't do.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:40.480
Machine Learning is not embodied and generally&nbsp;
cannot act to generate their own training data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.280
It just looks at data and&nbsp;
makes statistical predictions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.760
detects patterns - it doesn't try out&nbsp;
things to find causal relationships.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.480
So, why is that even important? If we just&nbsp;
want to make some predictions on known data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.480
we're fine without causality. But as soon&nbsp;
as we want to use deeper understanding of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:00.960
the domain and find truly invariant&nbsp;
features, like by suggesting actions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.280
we need to learn causal structures,&nbsp;
and not just statistical correlations.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:10.160
We may not want our house robot of the future to&nbsp;
actually have to drop our favorite vase to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.320
out that dropping it causes it to break,&nbsp;
and not breaking it causes it to drop.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:18.960
If you want to dive deeper into that topic,&nbsp;
we'll leave you a link to a great talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:22.560
in the description below and rather&nbsp;
leave the technical parts to the experts.

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:27.840
In case that was nothing new to you: what do&nbsp;
you think? Let us know in the comments whether,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:33.520
after transformers being one of the latest&nbsp;
big trends, now causality is... ALL YOU NEED.

