00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:37.360
[Manifold Hypothesis]&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.400
And lastly, we have two questions&nbsp;
that are somewhat related:&nbsp;

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:44.240
First of all, Letitia wanted to know&nbsp;
what the manifold hypothesis actually is.&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.920
So, a manifold, for starters, is any topological&nbsp;
space where the surroundings of each point are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.800
similar to the Euclidean space. More simply&nbsp;
put, it's a space where for every local point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.880
we can describe it's neighburhood through a&nbsp;
chart, which is just a flat representation.

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:03.920
For example, for a sphere like the earth or a&nbsp;
coffee mug, if we zoom in enough, we have a flat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:09.920
surface that we can create a map for; we can no&nbsp;
longer see the curvature. So this is a manifold!

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:14.400
Now the manifold hypothesis actually states&nbsp;
that complex data from the real world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:17.040
like photos or handwritten digits – that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.760
high dimensional data with lots of pixels – can&nbsp;
be compressed into a lower dimensional form.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:29.440
What that means is that for these 28-by-28&nbsp;
pixel wide hand written digits, we can express&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:35.600
all plausible hand written digits through far&nbsp;
less values than these 28-by-28 color values.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:40.400
For example, we could reduce that to some&nbsp;
information on what handwritten digit it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:45.280
and some piece of information that describes&nbsp;
how thick and skewed and so on this number is.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:49.120
In result, we'd get a representation&nbsp;
that's probably a lot lower dimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:52.240
than these 784 dimensions of our pixel space.

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:59.760
So, we then have a low dimensional representation&nbsp;
of our data that lies in the high dimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:04.240
image space. This lower dimensional&nbsp;
structure, this more simple representation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:06.560
that is the data manifold.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:09.440
And what happens to the rest&nbsp;
of the high dimensional space?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.200
Well, it is populated with data that&nbsp;
does not make sense in the real world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:18.240
In the image space, this would consist of all the&nbsp;
images that are pure noise in all configurations.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.800
In other words, the manifold hypothesis tells us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:25.040
that natural data is more structured&nbsp;
and constrained than one would expect:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:30.400
that the possibilities to generate implausible,&nbsp;
non-existent data points are far more numerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:33.760
than natural data which has a&nbsp;
certain simplicity attached to it.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:39.520
So to summarize: the manifold hypothesis&nbsp;
just states that real world data, like these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:44.800
handwritten digits, is actually lower-dimensional&nbsp;
than the original real-world space it lives in;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:50.640
here the 784 pixel values. The same of&nbsp;
course holds for more complex examples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:53.600
like for pictures of cats or&nbsp;
representations of geometry.

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:57.120
[Denoising Autoencoder]
A question tightly linked to the manifold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:02.720
hypothesis is on what a denoising autoencoder&nbsp;
actually does. For a denoising autoencoder, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:07.120
train an autoencoder on some data, that is, you&nbsp;
feed data through a neural network that at some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:12.800
point has a smaller capacity than the input and&nbsp;
try to keep as much information alive as possible.

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:16.720
Now this should already sound&nbsp;
familiar to the manifold&nbsp;

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:20.000
hypothesis: an autoencoder with&nbsp;
a small bottleneck learns a lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.240
dimensional representation of the input data.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:26.720
A noisy autoencoder then gets an&nbsp;
input that is actually corrupted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:30.160
for example, for handwritten digits, we&nbsp;
apply some noise to the image pixels.

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:35.120
When we then encode- and decode that and&nbsp;
now train such that the autoencoder does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:39.280
reconstruct the input, this is removing&nbsp;
some part of the noise, because for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:43.920
high-dimensional input of the image, only the&nbsp;
lower dimensional part with actual meaning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:47.920
the part without the noise, the real&nbsp;
data manifold, is actually learned.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:52.000
And that is because it is just way more&nbsp;
complicated to learn random noise patterns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:53.600
rather than meaningful data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:57.600
and it is much more complicated to&nbsp;
fit that through the tiny bottleneck.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:01.040
informally speaking, what this&nbsp;
actually does for our example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:05.200
is to learn the manifold of plausible&nbsp;
handwritten digits here inside the bottleneck.

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:09.920
For a noisy input that is not on that manifold,&nbsp;
the encoding process then just projects that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:14.560
back onto the learned manifold, because that is&nbsp;
all that the autoencoder can actually memorize.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:17.360
And by then decoding that,&nbsp;
we get a less noisy output.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.720
There's some great and easy to&nbsp;
understand paper in the description below&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:23.360
with soome more thoughts about&nbsp;
the manifold, go check it out.

